oney  the buzz was also fueled by social media  star Founders who posted their  accomplishments online so what would  happen is a SPAC would announce a merger  and then creators like tremath  palhapatia or others would promote the  merger on CNBC it's a business that I  think will become extremely valuable  they would tweet about it and individual  investors would jump on the bandwagon  and these things would start trending  the hype helped Fuel stock price gains  for companies like Lucid Virgin Galactic  and Nikola and spacfone Founders  generated average returns of more than  eight times their investment in 2020.  there is a period in time that I tracked  where every single company that  announced a SPAC merger shares of that  spec went up but the flood of  fundraising and ipoing didn't last after  a record month when spax raised 36  billion dollars things took a turn even  academics journalists people like me  were writing articles saying like this  won't end well because we have a lot of  data showing this historically never  ends well but people weren't really  listening in that moment so just became  like a firestorm one of the two reasons  behind the fall was that a few electric  vehicle startups had promised success in  their business projections misleading  their investors take a rival which sells  electric buses it said it would go from  zero Revenue to 14 billion dollars in  just three years arrivals president  explained the projections by saying that  2021 was a turning point for Rapid  adoption of electric vehicles in many  cases what was under the hood was not  very pretty and not as bold and like  spicy as people thought at the time of  the merger announcement so there is a  lot of bad information frankly out there  too that was pushing people to jump on  the bandwagon this caught the eye of  regulators and led to the second reason  behind the fall in April the SEC warned  that some spacs might have to restate  their financial results because at the  end of the day we need to ensure that  investors are protected both reasons  spooked investors share prices declined  and many investors withdrew their money  rather than hold on to shares of  startups after they completed spec deals  after the summer of 2021 companies  started to think twice before going  public with us back a lot of companies  are really second guessing whether they  want to do spec deals as well so that  kind of positive effect that made specs  boom is now working in Reverse where now  the negative connotation is making  everyone second guess things activity  stayed at a relatively low consistent  rate for the next year then in March  2022 the SEC proposed a set of  regulations that would make it harder  for spax to raise money from investors  and execute mergers IBS staff for  recommendations about how we might  update our roles so that investors are  better informed about the fees the costs  the conflicts that may exist with these  specs the goal of the regulations to  make companies disclose information more  like a traditional IPO but a lot of the  damage had already been done by that  point that sentiment though that  Regulators are stepping in and going to  regulate these things more that's also  had a chilling effect for sure in the  stock market by May about 90 percent of  specs that merged during the 2020 boom  were trading below their initial listing  price analysts estimate that if the  current pace of mergers continues a  large percentage of the roughly 280  specs that have deadlines in the first  quarter of 2023 won't find a match  recently SPAC activity is at a low back  activity is basically drawn to a  standstill in July no new specs raise  money early last year there were  basically tens of new specs every week  the market along with the broader stock  market and market for newly public  companies is definitely reversed a lot  after interest rates climbed in the  spring speculative Investments are  losing favor and valuations for